\begin_layout Subsection
Perfect Encryption [Shannon 48]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Encryption scheme
\end_layout

\end_inset

An encryption scheme is a pair of algorithms 
\begin_inset Formula $(E,D)$
\end_inset

 with message space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}$
\end_inset

, key space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{K}$
\end_inset

, and ciphertext space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{C}$
\end_inset

. For every 
\begin_inset Formula $K\in\mathcal{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M\in\mathcal{M}$
\end_inset

, encryption outputs 
\begin_inset Formula $E_K(M)\in\mathcal{C}$
\end_inset

, and correctness requires 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
D_K(E_K(M))=M.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

