\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Shannon's lower bound
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:shannon-lower-bound"

\end_inset

 For 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-bit messages, any perfectly secure encryption scheme requires at least 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 bits of key length and at least 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 bits of communication. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Fix a ciphertext 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 that can occur. Perfect secrecy implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 can occur for every message. For each fixed key, correctness permits at most one message to decrypt from 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

. Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mathcal{K}|\geq|\mathcal{M}|=2^n$
\end_inset

, so the key length is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For every fixed key 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

, correctness makes 
\begin_inset Formula $m\mapsto E_K(m)$
\end_inset

 injective. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mathcal{C}|\geq|\mathcal{M}|=2^n$
\end_inset

, so the ciphertext communication is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 bits. 
\end_layout

