Reconstruction Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Reconstruction and correctness
\end_layout

\end_inset

For every coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\{i_1,\ldots,i_t\}\subseteq[n]$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 parties, the reconstruction function is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D}_T:(s_{i_1},\ldots,s_{i_t})\longmapsto s.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Correctness requires that for every 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r\in\mathcal{R}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D}_T\bigl((\mathcal{E}_i(s;r))_{i\in T}\bigr)=s.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Linear Secret Sharing and Hiding Properties of Linear Maps
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:linear-secret-sharing"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

 be a finite field, let 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathbb{F}^{n\times k}$
\end_inset

 be a public matrix, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{F}^n$
\end_inset

 be a public vector. For a scalar message 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

 and a random column vector 
\begin_inset Formula $r\xleftarrow{R}\mathbb{F}^k$
\end_inset

, define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
E_r(s)=A\cdot r+s\cdot v.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Linear separation
\end_layout

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $v\notin\operatorname{Col}(A)$
\end_inset

, then there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{F}^n$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
w^TA=0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
w^Tv\neq0.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 does not belong to the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{Col}(A)$
\end_inset

, there is a linear functional that vanishes on 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{Col}(A)$
\end_inset

 but does not vanish on 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

. Writing this functional as 
\begin_inset Formula $x\mapsto w^Tx$
\end_inset

 gives the required vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Recoverability
\end_layout

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $v\notin\operatorname{Col}(A)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 is recoverable from 
\begin_inset Formula $E_r(s)$
\end_inset

 by a linear decoder. Let 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
u=\frac{w}{w^Tv}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Then, for a ciphertext 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
u^Tc=\frac{w^Tc}{w^Tv},
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w^TA=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w^Tv\neq0$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For 
\begin_inset Formula $c=E_r(s)=Ar+sv$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
u^Tc
=\frac{w^T(Ar+sv)}{w^Tv}
=\frac{w^TAr+s(w^Tv)}{w^Tv}
=s.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The map 
\begin_inset Formula $c\mapsto u^Tc$
\end_inset

 is linear over 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Perfect hiding
\end_layout

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\operatorname{Col}(A)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E_r(s)$
\end_inset

 perfectly hides 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. Formally, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $s,s'\in\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

 and uniform 
\begin_inset Formula $r\xleftarrow{R}\mathbb{F}^k$
\end_inset

, the random variables 
\begin_inset Formula $E_r(s)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_r(s')$
\end_inset

 are identically distributed. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\operatorname{Col}(A)$
\end_inset

, there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mathbb{F}^k$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $v=Az$
\end_inset

. Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
E_r(s)=Ar+sv=A(r+sz).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

For every fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, the map 
\begin_inset Formula $r\mapsto r+sz$
\end_inset

 is a bijection on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{F}^k$
\end_inset

. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $r+sz$
\end_inset

 is uniform whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 is uniform, so the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_r(s)$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary
The one-time pad proof is an instance of the hiding property of a non-degenerate linear mapping. 
\end_layout

