\begin_layout Subsection
Lower Bound on Share Size
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 1, Exercise 3
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $t<n$
\end_inset

 be positive integers and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 be a finite secret space of size at least 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

. Consider an arbitrary 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-out-of-
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 secret-sharing scheme 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathcal{E}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{R}
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{S}_1\times\cdots\times\mathcal{S}_n.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Then, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\log|\mathcal{S}_i|\geq\log|\mathcal{S}|.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

That is, the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th share size is at least as large as the length of the secret. Logarithms are taken to base 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Fix 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[n]$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $t<n$
\end_inset

, choose a set 
\begin_inset Formula $T\subseteq[n]\setminus\{i\}$
\end_inset

 of size 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

. Choose a secret 
\begin_inset Formula $s_0\in\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 and a value 
\begin_inset Formula $a_T\in\prod_{j\in T}\mathcal{S}_j$
\end_inset

 that occurs with positive probability as the shares of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 when the secret is 
\begin_inset Formula $s_0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Perfect privacy implies that the distribution of the shares of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is identical for every secret. Hence, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

, there is some randomness 
\begin_inset Formula $r_s\in\mathcal{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\bigl(\mathcal{E}_j(s;r_s)\bigr)_{j\in T}=a_T.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\phi(s)=\mathcal{E}_i(s;r_s)\in\mathcal{S}_i.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is injective. If 
\begin_inset Formula $s\neq s'$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(s)=\phi(s')$
\end_inset

, then the authorized coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T\cup\{i\}$
\end_inset

 receives exactly the same tuple of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 shares in the two executions. Correctness would therefore require the deterministic reconstruction function 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{T\cup\{i\}}$
\end_inset

 to output both 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s'$
\end_inset

, a contradiction. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is injective, so 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
|\mathcal{S}_i|\geq|\mathcal{S}|.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Taking base-
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 logarithms proves the theorem. 
\end_layout

