\begin_layout Subsection
Problem Formulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(x[1],\ldots,x[N])\in\{0,1\}^N$
\end_inset

 be replicated among 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 non-communicating servers. A private information retrieval (PIR) protocol allows a user holding an index 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[N]$
\end_inset

 to recover 
\begin_inset Formula $x[i]$
\end_inset

. Information- theoretic 1-privacy requires the marginal distribution of the query received by each server to be independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The communication complexity is the sum of the lengths of all queries and answers. The full-download protocol communicates 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 bits. A PIR protocol has sublinear communication if its communication complexity is 
\begin_inset Formula $o(N)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[Chor–Goldreich–Kushilevitz–Sudan 95]
\end_layout

