 A computationally 1-private 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-server PIR protocol is a family of probabilistic polynomial-time algorithms indexed by a security parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
Q&:(1^\lambda,N,i;r)\longmapsto(q_1,\ldots,q_k),\\
A_s&:(1^\lambda,x,q_s)\longmapsto a_s,
\qquad s\in[k],\\
D&:(1^\lambda,N,i,r,a_1,\ldots,a_k)\longmapsto z,
\end{align*}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\{0,1\}^N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[N]$
\end_inset

. The protocol satisfies: 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
Correctness.
\emph default
 For every 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda,N,x,i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[D(1^\lambda,N,i,R,a_1,\ldots,a_k)=x[i]]
\geq1-\operatorname{negl}(\lambda),
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(q_1,\ldots,q_k)=Q(1^\lambda,N,i;R)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_s=A_s(1^\lambda,x,q_s)$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
Computational 1-privacy.
\emph default
 For every polynomially bounded 
\begin_inset Formula $N=N(\lambda)$
\end_inset

, every 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in[k]$
\end_inset

, and every 
\begin_inset Formula $i_0,i_1\in[N]$
\end_inset

, the ensembles 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\{Q_s(1^\lambda,N,i_0;R)\}_{\lambda\in\mathbb{N}}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\{Q_s(1^\lambda,N,i_1;R)\}_{\lambda\in\mathbb{N}}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

are computationally indistinguishable. 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Separator plain

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Distinguishing gap
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X,Y$
\end_inset

 be distributions over a common finite set 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $A:\Omega\to\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

. Define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Delta_A(X,Y)
=\left|\Pr[A(X)=1]-\Pr[A(Y)=1]\right|.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The distributions are perfectly indistinguishable if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_A(X,Y)=0$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

; they are 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

-statistically indistinguishable if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_A(X,Y)\leq\epsilon$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

; and they are 
\begin_inset Formula $(t,\epsilon)$
\end_inset

-computationally indistinguishable if this inequality holds for every probabilistic algorithm 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 of running time at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Separator plain

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Negligible function
\end_layout

\end_inset

A function 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}$
\end_inset

 is negligible if for every constant 
\begin_inset Formula $c>0$
\end_inset

 there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_c$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mu(\lambda)<\lambda^{-c}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

for every 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq\lambda_c$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Separator plain

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Asymptotic indistinguishability
\end_layout

\end_inset

Distribution ensembles 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\{X_\lambda\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=\{Y_\lambda\}$
\end_inset

 are statistically indistinguishable, written 
\begin_inset Formula $X\approx_sY$
\end_inset

, if 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\max_A\Delta_A(X_\lambda,Y_\lambda)=\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

They are computationally indistinguishable, written 
\begin_inset Formula $X\approx_cY$
\end_inset

, if for every probabilistic polynomial-time 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Delta_A(X_\lambda,Y_\lambda)=\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Subexponential and exponential variants replace polynomial-time distinguishers by distinguishers of running time 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\lambda^\delta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\delta\lambda)$
\end_inset

, respectively, for a fixed constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Closure properties
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let all ensembles below be efficiently samplable. Computational indistinguishability satisfies: 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Transitivity: if 
\begin_inset Formula $X\approx_cY$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\approx_cZ$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $X\approx_cZ$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Closure under efficient computation: if 
\begin_inset Formula $X\approx_cY$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is PPT, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(X)\approx_cF(Y)$
\end_inset

. 
