\backslash
end{protocol}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell\neq i$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $s^0_\ell=s^1_\ell$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b^0_\ell=b^1_\ell$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{0,\ell}+Q_{1,\ell}=0$
\end_inset

. For 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
Q_{0,i}+Q_{1,i}
=G(s^0_i)+G(s^1_i)+\Delta
=e_j.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_0+Q_1=e_{i,j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For the privacy of 
\begin_inset Formula $K_0$
\end_inset

, replace the hidden value 
\begin_inset Formula $G(s^1_i)$
\end_inset

 by a uniform vector in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{F}_2^M$
\end_inset

. PRG security implies that this change is computationally indistinguishable, and in the resulting hybrid 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 is uniform and independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(i,j)$
\end_inset

. All seeds in 
\begin_inset Formula $K_0$
\end_inset

 and the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $b^0$
\end_inset

 are independently uniform. The argument for 
\begin_inset Formula $K_1$
\end_inset

 is symmetric, using the hidden seed 
\begin_inset Formula $s^0_i$
\end_inset

 and the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $b^1$
\end_inset

 is uniform. Hence each key distribution is computationally independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(i,j)$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Balancing 
\begin_inset Formula $L\lambda$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M=N/L$
\end_inset

 gives key length 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\sqrt{N\lambda})$
\end_inset

.
