\begin_layout Standard
Correction word for the chosen branch
\end_layout

\end_inset

Suppose the two states are 
\begin_inset Formula $(S,b)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(S',b')$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $b\neq b'$
\end_inset

. The left children are equal if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
L=\frac{G_L(S)-G_L(S')}{b'-b}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The right children are unequal if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
R\neq\frac{G_R(S)-G_R(S')}{b'-b}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For the left children, equality is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
G_L(S)+bL-\left(G_L(S')+b'L\right)=0,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

which rearranges to the displayed value of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $b'-b\neq0$
\end_inset

. The same calculation characterizes equality on the right branch. If the two input states are equal, both evaluators apply identical expansions and identical corrections; hence both pairs of children remain equal. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The DPF key-generation algorithm samples the two root states and computes one correction word at each level so that the invariant follows the path 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_d)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

