\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Exam A 2025, Question 3
\end_layout

\end_inset

With the Hadamard PIR, the protocol is correct and protects the index from each of the two parties answering PIR queries. It is not passively private against the querying party, is not passively 2-private, and is not secure against one active corruption. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Correctness follows from PIR correctness and 
\begin_inset Formula $y[i]\oplus r[i]=(r[i]\oplus x[i])\oplus r[i]=x[i]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The view of Alice contains a uniformly distributed Hadamard query, and the view of Carol contains the uniformly distributed mask 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and a uniformly distributed query. These distributions do not reveal 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Privacy against Bob fails even when Bob follows the protocol. For the Hadamard answer map, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
a_1=\langle y,q_1\rangle
=\langle r,q_1\rangle+\langle x,q_1\rangle.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Since Bob knows 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_1$
\end_inset

, he recovers 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle x,q_1\rangle$
\end_inset

. To obtain an explicit distinguishing pair, fix 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq i$
\end_inset

 and compare 
\begin_inset Formula $x=0^n$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x'=e_j$
\end_inset

. Both inputs have prescribed output zero at location 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

. For 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, the recovered inner product is always zero; for 
\begin_inset Formula $x'$
\end_inset

, it equals the uniform bit 
\begin_inset Formula $q_1[j]$
\end_inset

. Hence no simulator given only 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x[i]$
\end_inset

 can reproduce Bob's view.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The protocol is not 2-private. Alice and Carol together see both Hadamard queries and compute 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
q_1\oplus q_2=e_i,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

revealing 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

. Bob and Carol together know both 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, and therefore recover 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
x=r\oplus y.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The protocol is not actively 1-secure. A malicious Bob need not generate valid queries for a unit vector. He can choose Hadamard queries satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
q_1\oplus q_2=e_1\oplus e_2,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

and thereby recover 
\begin_inset Formula $y[1]\oplus y[2]$
\end_inset

. Since he knows 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

, he obtains the unauthorized linear combination 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
x[1]\oplus x[2]
=(y[1]\oplus y[2])\oplus(r[1]\oplus r[2]).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Moreover, a malicious Carol can inject errors in a way that is unavailable in the ideal functionality. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
PIR privacy protects an honestly generated index query from an individual server. It does not by itself enforce valid client queries, protect against server collusion, or provide active MPC security. 
\end_layout

