\begin_layout Subsection
Deterministic CDS Protocols
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Deterministic CDS
\end_layout

\end_inset

A deterministic two-party CDS is a CDS in which Alice and Bob do not share common randomness. Thus their messages are deterministic functions 
\begin_inset Formula $A(x,s)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B(y,s)$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 3, Exercise 1: characterization
\end_layout

\end_inset

A predicate 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f:X_1\times X_2\longrightarrow\{0,1\}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

has a deterministic two-party CDS if and only if every 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 in its truth table lies in an all-
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 row or in an all-
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 column. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For necessity, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x,y)=1$
\end_inset

, but row 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 contains a zero and column 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 contains a zero. Choose 
\begin_inset Formula $y_0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f(x,y_0)=0,
\qquad
f(x_0,y)=0.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Privacy at 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y_0)$
\end_inset

 implies that the deterministic message 
\begin_inset Formula $A(x,s)$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. Privacy at 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_0,y)$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $B(y,s)$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. Therefore the pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(A(x,s),B(y,s))$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, which contradicts correctness at the 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

-entry 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For sufficiency, suppose every 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

-entry has the stated property. Alice sends 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 when row 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is all 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and sends a fixed constant otherwise. Bob sends 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 when column 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is all 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and sends a fixed constant otherwise. Charlie, who knows 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

, outputs the message belonging to an all-
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 row or column when 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x,y)=1$
\end_inset

. At a 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

-entry, neither its row nor its column is all 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, so both messages are fixed constants and reveal nothing about 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Impossibility for AND
\end_layout

\end_inset

The predicate 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f(x_1,x_2)=x_1\wedge x_2
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

cannot be realized by a deterministic two-party CDS protocol. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The unique 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

-entry 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)$
\end_inset

 lies neither in an all-
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 row nor in an all-
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 column. The characterization theorem applies. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Trivial predicate
\end_layout

\end_inset

A predicate 
\begin_inset Formula $f:X_1\times X_2\to\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

 is trivial if either 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X_1$
\end_inset

 and every 
\begin_inset Formula $y,z\in X_2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x,y)=f(x,z)$
\end_inset

; or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X_2$
\end_inset

 and every 
\begin_inset Formula $y,z\in X_1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y,x)=f(z,x)$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Every predicate satisfying either condition has a deterministic CDS: the sender whose input determines 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 transmits 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 on accepting inputs and a fixed constant on rejecting inputs. The predicate OR also has a deterministic CDS by the characterization theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Conditional Enforcement of PIR Query Validity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(v,r)=(a,b)$
\end_inset

 be the query algorithm of a two-server PIR. Define the query-validity predicate 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
V(a,b)=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists v,r\text{ such that }Q(v,r)=(a,b).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Conditional release on well-formed queries
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 be any server-side value and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_V$
\end_inset

 be a CDS for 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 whose secret space contains 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

. If the two servers use 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b)$
\end_inset

 as their CDS inputs and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 as the CDS secret, then the receiver reconstructs 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b)$
\end_inset

 in the image of 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

, while the transcript is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b)$
\end_inset

 outside the image of 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b)=Q(v,r)$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $(v,r)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $V(a,b)=1$
\end_inset

 and CDS correctness gives reconstruction. Otherwise 
\begin_inset Formula $V(a,b)=0$
\end_inset

, and CDS privacy makes the joint message distribution independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the Hadamard PIR, the validity predicate is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
V(a,b)=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 a+b\in\{e_1,\ldots,e_N\}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Simple Closure Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Closure under AND
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Closure under AND
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:cds-and"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\operatorname{CDS}(f\wedge g)
\leq
\operatorname{CDS}(f)+\operatorname{CDS}(g).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume the secret space is a finite abelian group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

. Sample 
\begin_inset Formula $S_1\xleftarrow{R}G$
\end_inset

 and set 
\begin_inset Formula $S_2=s-S_1$
\end_inset

. Run the two CDS protocols with independent randomness and secrets 
\begin_inset Formula $S_1,S_2$
\end_inset

. If both predicates accept, the decoder reconstructs 
\begin_inset Formula $S_1+S_2=s$
\end_inset

. If, for example, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x,y)=0$
\end_inset

, the first transcript is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $S_1$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $S_1$
\end_inset

 is uniform, the joint transcript is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 even after conditioning on the second transcript. The case 
\begin_inset Formula $g(x,y)=0$
\end_inset

 is symmetric. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Closure under OR
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Closure under OR
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:cds-or"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\operatorname{CDS}(f\vee g)
\leq
\operatorname{CDS}(f)+\operatorname{CDS}(g).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Run the CDS protocols for 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 with the same secret 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and independent randomness. If either predicate accepts, the corresponding decoder reconstructs 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. If both reject, each transcript distribution is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

; independence of the protocol randomness implies that their joint distribution is also independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

