\begin_layout Subsubsection
Input Gates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Input sharing preserves the wire invariant
\end_layout

\end_inset

For an Alice input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, Alice chooses random 
\begin_inset Formula $x_2$
\end_inset

, sends it to Bob, and keeps 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
x_1=x-x_2.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

For a Bob input 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, Bob chooses random 
\begin_inset Formula $y_1$
\end_inset

, sends it to Alice, and keeps 
\begin_inset Formula $y_2=y-y_1$
\end_inset

. In both cases the two shares sum to the input value. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Addition and Subtraction Gates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Linear gates are local
\end_layout

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $w=x-z$
\end_inset

, the parties locally compute 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
w_1=x_1-z_1,
\qquad
w_2=x_2-z_2.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
w_1+w_2=(x_1-z_1)+(x_2-z_2)=x-z=w,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

so the invariant is maintained. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Multiplication Gates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Multiplication gate from two OT calls
\end_layout

\end_inset

Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x=x_1+x_2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z=z_1+z_2$
\end_inset

. The desired product is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
v=(x_1+x_2)(z_1+z_2)
=x_1z_1+x_1z_2+x_2z_1+x_2z_2.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The two cross terms are computed using two OT calls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
Over a finite field 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

, Alice samples 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

 and acts as OT sender with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
M_s=x_1s-\alpha,
\qquad s\in\mathbb{F}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Bob chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $z_2$
\end_inset

 and receives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
U=x_1z_2-\alpha.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Bob samples 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

 and acts as OT sender with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
N_s=x_2s-\beta.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Alice chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $z_1$
\end_inset

 and receives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
V=x_2z_1-\beta.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

They set 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
v_1=x_1z_1+V+\alpha,
\qquad
v_2=x_2z_2+U+\beta.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
v_1+v_2=(x_1+x_2)(z_1+z_2).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_1,v_2)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the wire-sharing invariant. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Output Phase
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Output reconstruction
\end_layout

\end_inset

If the output wire has shares 
\begin_inset Formula $t_1,t_2$
\end_inset

, Alice sends 
\begin_inset Formula $t_1$
\end_inset

 to Bob, who outputs 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
t=t_1+t_2.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Two-party GMW
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:two-party-gmw"

\end_inset

 The GMW construction gives a two-party protocol for every efficiently computable function with perfect privacy in the OT-hybrid model. Its communication and computation are linear in circuit size. It extends to many parties and arithmetic circuits over larger fields. Its round complexity is the multiplicative depth of the circuit. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Correctness follows by induction over a topological ordering of the circuit, using the four gate claims above. For privacy, every intermediate wire is represented by a uniform additive sharing conditioned only on its value. The ideal OT calls expose only the selected masked cross terms. A simulator therefore samples all intermediate shares and OT outputs uniformly and fixes only the final share to match the prescribed output. Each multiplication gate uses a constant number of OTs, and gates in the same multiplicative layer run in parallel. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
From Decomposable Randomized Encodings to Protocols
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A decomposable randomized encoding, also called a multiparty PSM, has the form 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g(x,r)=\bigl(g_0(r),g_1(x_1,r),\ldots,g_n(x_n,r)\bigr).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose Alice holds 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1,\ldots,x_{10}$
\end_inset

 and Bob holds 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11},\ldots,x_{20}$
\end_inset

. Alice samples 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and sends 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g_0(r),g_1(x_1,r),\ldots,g_{10}(x_{10},r).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

For every Bob input bit 
\begin_inset Formula $x_j$
\end_inset

, Alice acts as OT sender with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\bigl(g_j(0,r),g_j(1,r)\bigr),
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

and Bob selects 
\begin_inset Formula $x_j$
\end_inset

. Bob receives all components of the encoding and decodes 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

. The protocol has one round of OT calls, generalizes to the multiparty setting, and permits the offline message 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0(r)$
\end_inset

 to be sent before the inputs are available.
\end_layout

