\begin_layout Section
Passive Multi-Party Computation in the Plain and OT-Hybrid Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Multi-Party Protocol Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 parties 
\begin_inset Formula $P_1,\ldots,P_n$
\end_inset

. Party 
\begin_inset Formula $P_i$
\end_inset

 holds an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_i$
\end_inset

 and private randomness 
\begin_inset Formula $r_i$
\end_inset

. The parties are connected by private point-to-point channels. In the plain model these are the only available channels; in the OT-hybrid model every pair of parties may additionally call an ideal oblivious-transfer functionality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In round 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

, party 
\begin_inset Formula $P_j$
\end_inset

 sends a message 
\begin_inset Formula $M^{\ell}_{j:k}$
\end_inset

 to party 
\begin_inset Formula $P_k$
\end_inset

 as a function of its current view. The view of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_j$
\end_inset

 after round 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

 contains 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
x_j,\quad r_j,\quad
\left(M^{h}_{k:j}:k\in[n],\ 1\leq h\leq\ell\right).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Correctness
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f(x_1,\ldots,x_n)=(f_1(x_1,\ldots,x_n),\ldots,
f_n(x_1,\ldots,x_n)).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

A protocol is correct if every party 
\begin_inset Formula $P_i$
\end_inset

 outputs 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
y_i=f_i(x_1,\ldots,x_n).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Separator plain

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Passive 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-privacy
\end_layout

\end_inset

For a coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T\subseteq[n]$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\operatorname{View}_T(x_1,\ldots,x_n)
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

denote the joint view of the parties in 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

. A protocol is passively 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-private if, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 of size at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, there is a simulator 
\begin_inset Formula $S_T$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\operatorname{View}_T(x_1,\ldots,x_n)
\equiv
S_T(x_T,f_T(x_1,\ldots,x_n)).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Full privacy means privacy against every proper coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T\subsetneq[n]$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The REAL/IDEAL Paradigm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the REAL execution, the parties run the protocol and an adversary sees the views of the corrupted parties. In the IDEAL execution, every party sends its input to a trusted party, which computes 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and returns the prescribed outputs. Security requires the REAL and IDEAL distributions to be identical in the perfect passive setting, or indistinguishable in the corresponding statistical or computational setting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Perfect passive security
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "def:passive-mpc-security"

\end_inset

 A protocol securely computes a deterministic functionality 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 against a passive adversary if, for every corrupted coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, the REAL execution is identically distributed to an IDEAL execution generated from 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_T,f_T(x))$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For deterministic functionalities, this formulation is equivalent to the view-simulation definition above.
\end_layout

