\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Correctness and privacy of the sum protocol
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:sum-protocol"

\end_inset

 The complete-graph sum protocol is correct and perfectly private against every proper passive coalition. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Correctness follows because every mask occurs once with a plus sign and once with a minus sign: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i
&=\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i
+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}r_{j,i}
-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}r_{i,j}\\
&=\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i.
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Fix a proper coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

. The simulator samples all randomness held or received by parties in 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 exactly as in the protocol and computes their broadcasts. It then samples all but one of the honest broadcasts uniformly and chooses the final honest broadcast so that the sum of all broadcasts is the prescribed output. This is the real distribution, since the masks on edges between honest parties make the honest broadcasts uniform subject only to their required sum. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The same protocol privately computes every linear function. It requires no oblivious transfer.
\end_layout

