\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Sum Protocol on an Arbitrary Graph
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G=([n],E)$
\end_inset

 be an undirected graph, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $N(i)$
\end_inset

 denote the neighbors of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

. Party 
\begin_inset Formula $P_i$
\end_inset

 sends a uniform 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 to every 
\begin_inset Formula $P_j$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in N(i)$
\end_inset

 and then broadcasts 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
c_i=x_i+
\sum_{j\in N(i)}r_{j,i}-
\sum_{j\in N(i)}r_{i,j}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

-robustness
\end_layout

\end_inset

For a corrupted coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T\subseteq[n]$
\end_inset

, write 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\overline{T}=[n]\setminus T.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The graph 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

-robust if the induced graph 
\begin_inset Formula $G[\overline{T}]$
\end_inset

 is connected. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 4, Exercise 3
\end_layout

\end_inset

The graph-based sum protocol is privately simulatable against the coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

-robust. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Correctness holds for every undirected graph, since 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j\in N(i)}r_{j,i}
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j\in N(i)}r_{i,j}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose first that 
\begin_inset Formula $G[\overline{T}]$
\end_inset

 is connected. The simulator samples all random values on edges incident to 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 and computes the broadcasts of the corrupted parties. Choose a spanning tree of 
\begin_inset Formula $G[\overline{T}]$
\end_inset

. The masks on its edges allow the honest broadcast vector to range uniformly over all vectors having the required sum. Consequently, the simulator samples all but one honest broadcast uniformly and fixes the last one using the public sum. This produces the real distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Conversely, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $G[\overline{T}]$
\end_inset

 is disconnected. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 be one connected component and let 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\overline{T}\setminus A$
\end_inset

. Define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\delta_A=
\sum_{\substack{i\in A\\j\in N(i)\cap T}}
(r_{j,i}-r_{i,j}).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Because no edge joins 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, all masks internal to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 cancel, and the coalition can compute 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\sum_{i\in A}c_i-\delta_A=\sum_{i\in A}x_i.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{i}\in A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{j}\in B$
\end_inset

. The two input vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\widetilde{i}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\widetilde{j}}$
\end_inset

 have the same corrupted inputs and the same global sum 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, but the displayed partial sum is 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 in the first execution and 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 in the second. Hence no simulator receiving only the corrupted inputs and the global output can generate both views. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary
The protocol is private against every coalition of size at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 exactly when deleting any 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 vertices leaves the graph connected. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Rushing Adversaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Rushing adversary
\end_layout

\end_inset

A rushing adversary may wait within a round, observe the honest parties' messages from that round, and only then choose the corrupted parties' messages for the same round. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 4, Exercise 4
\end_layout

\end_inset

The sum protocol is not secure against an active rushing adversary, even if only 
\begin_inset Formula $P_1$
\end_inset

 is corrupted. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The corrupted party may behave honestly in the first round. In the broadcast round it waits to observe 
\begin_inset Formula $c_2,\ldots,c_n$
\end_inset

 and then broadcasts 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\widehat{c}_1=-\sum_{i=2}^{n}c_i.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The reconstructed result is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\widehat{c}_1+\sum_{i=2}^{n}c_i=0,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

independently of the honest inputs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
This behavior cannot be simulated in the ideal execution. Consider two honest input vectors whose sums differ by 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 while the corrupted party's input and its view before the output are the same. In the real execution the adversary forces output 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 in both cases. In the ideal execution a single effective input for 
\begin_inset Formula $P_1$
\end_inset

 cannot make both distinct honest sums evaluate to 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

. Thus the REAL and IDEAL executions cannot agree. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Completeness of Functionalities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Complete functionality
\end_layout

\end_inset

A functionality is complete if access to it, together with private communication and local computation, suffices to securely realize oblivious transfer and hence every efficiently computable functionality. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Exam A 2025, Question 4(a)
\end_layout

\end_inset

The two-party functionality 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g(x_1,x_2)=(x_1\vee x_2,\bot)
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

is complete. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let the receiver hold the OT selection bit 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, and let the sender hold messages 
\begin_inset Formula $m_0,m_1$
\end_inset

. Make two calls in which the receiver supplies the first input: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g(s,m_0)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
g(1-s,m_1).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The receiver obtains 
\begin_inset Formula $s\vee m_0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-s)\vee m_1$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $s=0$
\end_inset

, the first value is 
\begin_inset Formula $m_0$
\end_inset

 and the second is 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

; if 
\begin_inset Formula $s=1$
\end_inset

, the first is 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and the second is 
\begin_inset Formula $m_1$
\end_inset

. Thus the receiver learns exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $m_s$
\end_inset

, while the sender receives no output and learns nothing about 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Exam A 2025, Question 4(b)
\end_layout

\end_inset

The public-output functionality 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g(x_1,(x_2,x_2'))=(x_1\wedge x_2)\oplus x_2'
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

is complete. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The second party chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $x_2'$
\end_inset

 uniformly. The public output is a one-time pad of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1\wedge x_2$
\end_inset

. The second party knows the pad and recovers the AND, whereas the first party learns nothing about it. Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 realizes the functionality 
\begin_inset Formula $g'$
\end_inset

 that delivers 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1\wedge x_2$
\end_inset

 only to the second party.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
To implement OT, make two calls 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g'(m_1,s)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
g'(m_0,1-s).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The receiver obtains 
\begin_inset Formula $m_1s$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_0(1-s)$
\end_inset

 and therefore recovers 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
m_1s\oplus m_0(1-s)=m_s.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The sender learns nothing about 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Exam A 2025, Question 4(c)
\end_layout

\end_inset

The public three-party functionality 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
g(x_1,x_2,x_3)=x_1\oplus x_2\oplus x_3
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

is not complete. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The functionality is securely realizable in the plain model by the private sum protocol over 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{F}_2$
\end_inset

. If it were complete, composing that plain protocol with a completeness reduction would yield a plain-model protocol for two-party AND. This contradicts the impossibility of privately computing two-party AND in the plain model. The fact that the functionality has three parties is not by itself sufficient: three-party AND is complete. 
\end_layout

