\begin_layout Subsection
Active Security
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An active, or malicious, adversary may arbitrarily deviate from the protocol. It may coordinate all corrupted parties, change their messages, send inconsistent shares, and choose values that do not correspond to any well-defined input.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Active 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-security
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "def:active-security"

\end_inset

 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 be an active adversary corrupting a coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 of at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 parties. A protocol actively 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-securely realizes a functionality 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 if there exists an ideal-model simulator 
\begin_inset Formula $S_T$
\end_inset

 such that, for every input vector 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_n),
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

the adversary's real view together with the outputs of the honest parties is identically distributed to the simulator's view and the honest outputs in the ideal execution with a trusted party. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the passive security of Definition
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:passive-mpc-security"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, the real-model adversary may deviate arbitrarily. In the ideal model, it can essentially only change the corrupted parties' inputs, independently of the honest inputs, and change the corrupted parties' outputs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Simple Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Broadcast
\end_layout

\end_inset

For 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f(x_1,\bot,\ldots,\bot)=(x_1,\ldots,x_1),
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

ordinary broadcast is secure against a corrupted receiver but not against a corrupted sender. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
A corrupted receiver has no secrecy requirement beyond learning 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1$
\end_inset

. A corrupted sender, however, may send different values to different receivers, whereas the ideal functionality supplies one consistent value to all honest parties. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Two-party sum
\end_layout

\end_inset

Consider 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f(x_1,x_2,\bot,\ldots,\bot)
=(x_1+x_2,x_1+x_2,\bot,\ldots,\bot).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The direct protocol is simulatable against a corrupted second sender who sends an illegal value, but it is not actively secure against a party that chooses its input after learning the other party's input. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
An illegal transmitted value can be interpreted as a fixed default input, such as zero. By contrast, choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_2$
\end_inset

, for example setting 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1=x_2$
\end_inset

, violates independence of inputs and cannot be reproduced in the ideal model. 
\end_layout

