\begin_layout Proof
Apply a 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-out-of-
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 additive sharing to 
\begin_inset Formula $(ab)$
\end_inset

 and independent 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-out-of-
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

 additive sharings to 
\begin_inset Formula $(bcd)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(acd)$
\end_inset

. Every authorized coalition contains a minimal authorized set and can reconstruct one copy of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. An unauthorized coalition lacks at least one uniform additive share from every copy. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
CNF secret sharing
\end_layout

\end_inset

For every maximal unauthorized set 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

, create one additive component 
\begin_inset Formula $r_U$
\end_inset

. Additively share 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 as the sum of all these components, and give 
\begin_inset Formula $r_U$
\end_inset

 to every party outside 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Exam solution: CNF share count
\end_layout

\end_inset

The maximal unauthorized sets are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
(bc),\qquad(bd),\qquad(ac),\qquad(ad),\qquad(cd).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Choose 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
s=r_{bc}+r_{bd}+r_{ac}+r_{ad}+r_{cd}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

and give each party the components whose subscript does not contain its name. Every component is stored twice, so the total share size is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
5\cdot2=10
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

field elements. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, server 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 stores 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
r_{bc},\quad r_{bd},\quad r_{cd}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
A six-element construction
\end_layout

\end_inset

The same access structure has a secret-sharing scheme with total share size 
\begin_inset Formula $6$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Independently use a 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-out-of-
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 sharing between 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 and a 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-out-of-
\begin_inset Formula $4$
\end_inset

 Shamir sharing among 
\begin_inset Formula $a,b,c,d$
\end_inset

. The first sharing uses two field elements and realizes the authorized set 
\begin_inset Formula $(ab)$
\end_inset

. The second uses four field elements and realizes every coalition of size at least three. The total is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
2+4=6.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
The single-element server trick
\end_layout

\end_inset

For every monotone access function, one distinguished server 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 can store only one field element. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1$
\end_inset

 indicate whether server 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 belongs to the coalition, and define the restricted access functions 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
f_0(x_2,\ldots,x_n)=f(0,x_2,\ldots,x_n),
\qquad
f_1(x_2,\ldots,x_n)=f(1,x_2,\ldots,x_n).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Choose a uniform 
\begin_inset Formula $r\in\mathbb{F}$
\end_inset

 and store only 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 at server 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

. Among the other servers, independently share 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 according to 
\begin_inset Formula $f_0$
\end_inset

 and share 
\begin_inset Formula $s-r$
\end_inset

 according to 
\begin_inset Formula $f_1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 is absent and the coalition is authorized, then 
\begin_inset Formula $f_0=1$
\end_inset

 and the coalition reconstructs 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 is present and the coalition is authorized, then 
\begin_inset Formula $f_1=1$
\end_inset

; it reconstructs 
\begin_inset Formula $s-r$
\end_inset

 and adds the stored value 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

. An unauthorized coalition without 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 either learns neither value or only the uniformly masked value 
\begin_inset Formula $s-r$
\end_inset

. An unauthorized coalition containing 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $f_1=0$
\end_inset

 and, by monotonicity, also 
\begin_inset Formula $f_0=0$
\end_inset

, so it cannot reconstruct either sharing. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the DNF scheme above, the same optimization can be viewed by letting server 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 hold random values 
\begin_inset Formula $r_1,r_2$
\end_inset

 and setting 
\begin_inset Formula $r_2=r_1$
\end_inset

. This reduces the share size of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 to one field element and is a special case of the 
\begin_inset Formula $f_0,f_1$
\end_inset

 construction.
\end_layout

