\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Soundness of Kilian's compilation
\end_layout

\end_inset

If the underlying PCP is sound and the hash family is collision resistant, then Kilian's protocol is a computationally sound succinct argument. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose a polynomial-size prover convinces the verifier on a false statement with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

. Fix a hash function 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 for which the conditional acceptance probability is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon/2$
\end_inset

. If every PCP location had one answer occurring with overwhelming conditional probability, those answers would define a fixed PCP proof accepted with non-negligible probability, contradicting PCP soundness. Consequently, there is an index 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for which both bit values occur in accepting openings with probability at least 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon/(4\ell)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

 is the PCP proof length.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Repeating the interaction 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\epsilon^{-1}\ell)$
\end_inset

 times yields two different valid authentication paths for the same index and root with constant probability. At the first tree node where the authenticated values differ, the two paths give a collision in 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, contradicting collision resistance. 
\end_layout

