, then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr_{r\xleftarrow{R}\mathbb{F}}
[\mathcal A(A,B,C;r)=0]
\geq1-\frac{n-1}{|\mathbb{F}|}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
There is an 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(AB)_i\neq C_i$
\end_inset

. If the algorithm accepts, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u_i=v_i$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\left(\sum_{k=1}^{n}a_{ik}b_{kj}-c_{ij}\right)r^{j-1}=0.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
p(X)=\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\left(\sum_{k=1}^{n}a_{ik}b_{kj}-c_{ij}\right)X^{j-1}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $(AB)_i\neq C_i$
\end_inset

, there is an index 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\sum_{k=1}^{n}a_{ik}b_{kj}-c_{ij}\neq0.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

This value is the coefficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{j-1}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is nonzero. Moreover, 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 has degree at most 
\begin_inset Formula $n-1$
\end_inset

. Therefore, by Schwartz–Zippel, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[\mathcal A(A,B,C;r)=1]
\leq\Pr[p(r)=0]
\leq\frac{n-1}{|\mathbb{F}|}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Taking the complement proves the claim. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The prover may send 
\begin_inset Formula $\bot$
\end_inset

, after which the verifier alone executes the test. This is an interactive proof for 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\mathrm{MM}}$
\end_inset

 with perfect completeness and soundness error 
\begin_inset Formula $(n-1)/|\mathbb{F}|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Zero-Error Interactive Proofs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 5, Exercise 2
\end_layout

\end_inset

If a language 
\begin_inset Formula $L\subseteq\Sigma^*$
\end_inset

 has an interactive proof with perfect completeness and perfect soundness, then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
L\in\mathsf{NP}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(P,V)$
\end_inset

 be such a proof, and suppose that on input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 the verifier uses 
\begin_inset Formula $r(|x|)$
\end_inset

 random bits. For a tape 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\in\{0,1\}^{r(|x|)}$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $V_\rho$
\end_inset

 denote the deterministic verifier obtained by fixing 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

's random tape to 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in L$
\end_inset

, perfect completeness says 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
1
&=\Pr_{\rho,R_P}[\langle P(R_P),V_\rho\rangle(x)=1]\\
&=2^{-r(|x|)}\sum_\rho
\Pr_{R_P}[\langle P(R_P),V_\rho\rangle(x)=1].
\end{align*}
\end_inset

Every summand lies in 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

, so every summand equals 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

. For 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notin L$
\end_inset

 and every cheating prover 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat P$
\end_inset

, perfect soundness gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
0
&=\Pr_{\rho,R_{\widehat P}}
[\langle\widehat P(R_{\widehat P}),V_\rho\rangle(x)=1]\\
&=2^{-r(|x|)}\sum_\rho
\Pr_{R_{\widehat P}}
[\langle\widehat P(R_{\widehat P}),V_\rho\rangle(x)=1].
\end{align*}
\end_inset

All terms are nonnegative, so each is 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

. Consequently, fixing any tape, say 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_0=0^{r(|x|)}$
\end_inset

, preserves both properties. Write 
\begin_inset Formula $V_0:=V_{\rho_0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose the protocol has 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 prover turns. For prover messages 
\begin_inset Formula $a_1,\ldots,a_k$
\end_inset

, use the notation 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\operatorname{Exec}_{V_0}(x;a_1,\ldots,a_k)
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

for the unique execution in which 
\begin_inset Formula $V_0$
\end_inset

 computes each of its messages from 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and the transcript so far, and receives 
\begin_inset Formula $a_i$
\end_inset

 on its 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th prover turn. Define an 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{NP}$
\end_inset

 verifier 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\mathsf{NP}}$
\end_inset

 whose witness is the polynomial-length sequence 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
w=a_1\sqcup\cdots\sqcup a_k.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

It simulates 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{Exec}_{V_0}(x;a_1,\ldots,a_k)$
\end_inset

 and accepts exactly when 
\begin_inset Formula $V_0$
\end_inset

 accepts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in L$
\end_inset

, the fixed-tape completeness statement above guarantees an accepting execution; its prover-message sequence is a witness accepted by 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\mathsf{NP}}$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notin L$
\end_inset

, every alleged witness 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 defines a deterministic cheating prover 
\begin_inset Formula $P_w^*$
\end_inset

: on the transcript prefixes occurring in the simulated execution it sends the next prescribed 
\begin_inset Formula $a_i$
\end_inset

, and on all other prefixes it acts arbitrarily. Hence 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
V_{\mathsf{NP}}(x,w)
=\langle P_w^*,V_0\rangle(x)=0
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

by fixed-tape perfect soundness. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\mathsf{NP}}$
\end_inset

 is a polynomial-time 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{NP}$
\end_inset

 verifier for 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sequential Repetition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi=(P,V)$
\end_inset

 have perfect completeness and soundness error 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

. The 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-fold sequential repetition 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 executes 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 on the same input 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 times in sequence with fresh verifier randomness and accepts only if every execution accepts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 5, Exercise 3
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 has perfect completeness and soundness error at most 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
2^{-n}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We prove the theorem by induction on 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\emph on
Base.
\emph default
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

, the protocol is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 and has perfect completeness and soundness error 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2=2^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\emph on
Step.
\emph default
 Assume the claim for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{(n)}$
\end_inset

. Perfect completeness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{(n+1)}$
\end_inset

 is formalized as follows. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 be the event that the first 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 repetitions accept and let 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 be the event that repetition 
\begin_inset Formula $n+1$
\end_inset

 accepts. For 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in L$
\end_inset

, the induction hypothesis gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pr[A]=1$
\end_inset

. Each repetition is a fresh execution of the honest protocol, so perfect completeness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pr[B\mid H=h]=1$
\end_inset

 after every history 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 of the first 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 repetitions having positive probability. Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[\Pi^{(n+1)}(x)=1]
=\Pr[A\cap B]=1.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For soundness, fix 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notin L$
\end_inset

 and an arbitrary cheating prover. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 be the set of histories 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 of the first 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 repetitions on which they all accept. By total probability, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[\Pi^{(n+1)}(x)=1]
=\sum_{h\in W}
\Pr[\Pi^{(n+1)}(x)=1\mid h]\Pr[h].
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

After fixing 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in W$
\end_inset

, the original prover together with its state after 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 induces a cheating strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $P_h^*$
\end_inset

 for the fresh 
\begin_inset Formula $(n+1)$
\end_inset

st execution. The base soundness bound therefore gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[\Pi^{(n+1)}(x)=1\mid h]\leq\frac12.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\Pr[\Pi^{(n+1)}(x)=1]
&\leq\frac12\sum_{h\in W}\Pr[h]\\
&=\frac12\Pr[\Pi^{(n)}(x)=1]\\
&\leq\frac12\cdot2^{-n}=2^{-(n+1)}.
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Quadratic Non-Residuosity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $N=pq$
\end_inset

 be the product of two distinct odd primes, and define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathbb{Z}_N^*=\{x\in\mathbb{Z}_N:\gcd(x,N)=1\}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Define 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathsf{QR}_N
=\{x\in\mathbb{Z}_N^*:\exists a\in\mathbb{Z}_N^*,\ a^2\equiv x
\pmod N\}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathsf{QNR}_N=\mathbb{Z}_N^*\setminus\mathsf{QR}_N.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbb{Z}_N^*,\cdot_N)$
\end_inset

 is a group and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\mathsf{QR}_N\trianglelefteq\mathbb{Z}_N^*.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Closure, associativity, and the identity 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 are immediate. For every 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{Z}_N^*$
\end_inset

, Bézout's identity gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha a+\beta N=1$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is the multiplicative inverse of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 modulo 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv a^2\pmod N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv b^2\pmod N$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
xy\equiv(ab)^2\pmod N,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

so 
\begin_inset Formula $xy\in\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

. Moreover, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
x^{-1}\equiv(a^{-1})^2\pmod N,
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

so 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{-1}\in\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

 is a subgroup; since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Z}_N^*$
\end_inset

 is abelian, it is normal. 
\end_layout

