\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 5, Exercise 4: completeness
\end_layout

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathsf{QNR}_N$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[\langle P,V\rangle(x)=1]=1.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $b=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z=y\in\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

, and the honest prover returns 
\begin_inset Formula $b'=0$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $b=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z=yx$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

 is a subgroup, multiplying a non-residue by a residue gives a non-residue, so the prover returns 
\begin_inset Formula $b'=1$
\end_inset

. In both cases, 
\begin_inset Formula $b'=b$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
HW 5, Exercise 4: soundness
\end_layout

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

, then for every 
\begin_inset Formula $P^*$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[\langle P^*,V\rangle(x)=1]\leq\frac12.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $b=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z=y$
\end_inset

 is uniform in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $b=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z=yx$
\end_inset

. Right multiplication by the fixed residue 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a bijection on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $yx$
\end_inset

 is also uniform in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

. More explicitly, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $z_0\in\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[Z=z_0\mid B=c]=\frac{1}{|\mathsf{QR}_N|}.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are independent. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be the internal randomness of an arbitrary prover 
\begin_inset Formula $P^*$
\end_inset

; it is sampled independently of 
\begin_inset Formula $(B,Y)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $(Z,R)$
\end_inset

 is also independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

. Since the reply 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
B'=P^*(x,Z;R)
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $(Z,R)$
\end_inset

, it is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

. As 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is uniform, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
\Pr[B'=B]
=\sum_{c\in\{0,1\}}\Pr[B'=c]\Pr[B=c]
=\frac12.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Thus every prover succeeds with probability exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Conditional failure of zero knowledge
\end_layout

\end_inset

Assume that quadratic residuosity remains hard for probabilistic polynomial-time algorithms even when they are given a valid common input 
\begin_inset Formula $(N,x)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathsf{QNR}_N$
\end_inset

 but not the factorization of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

. Then the quadratic non-residuosity protocol is not computational zero knowledge against malicious verifiers. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{Z}_N^*$
\end_inset

, define a malicious verifier 
\begin_inset Formula $V_w^*$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 hardwired into its description. It ignores the prescribed first-message algorithm and sends 
\begin_inset Formula $Z=w$
\end_inset

. The honest prover returns the deterministic bit 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
B'=\begin{cases}
0,&w\in\mathsf{QR}_N,\\
1,&w\notin\mathsf{QR}_N.
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

Thus the real view of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_w^*$
\end_inset

 contains the answer to the quadratic residuosity instance 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose, toward a contradiction, that a probabilistic polynomial-time simulator 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 produced a computationally indistinguishable view for every 
\begin_inset Formula $V_w^*$
\end_inset

. Run 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $(N,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_w^*$
\end_inset

, read the simulated reply bit, and use it to decide whether 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathsf{QR}_N$
\end_inset

. If the simulated bit differed from the real deterministic bit with nonnegligible probability, the polynomial-time test that reads this bit would distinguish the simulated and real views. Hence this procedure decides quadratic residuosity with all but negligible error, contradicting the stated hardness assumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The conclusion is therefore conditional on computational hardness. Without such an assumption, the interaction still demonstrates that a malicious verifier learns a residuosity bit, but this observation alone is not a formal impossibility proof for polynomial-time simulation. 
\end_layout

