\begin_layout Standard
GMW 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-coloring protocol
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:gmw-three-coloring"

\end_inset

 The protocol is sound with error 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
1-|E|^{-1}
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

and is zero knowledge. Sequential repetition reduces the soundness error while preserving zero knowledge. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Perfect completeness follows from the valid coloring. If the graph is not 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-colorable, perfect binding fixes one color for every vertex, and at least one edge is monochromatic. A uniform edge catches such an edge with probability at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1/|E|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For zero knowledge, the simulator guesses the verifier's challenge edge 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(u,v)$
\end_inset

, chooses distinct random colors for 
\begin_inset Formula $u,v$
\end_inset

, fixes arbitrary colors for all other vertices, and commits to them. It runs the verifier and outputs the view if the challenge equals 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

; otherwise it rewinds and tries again. Computational hiding makes unopened commitments indistinguishable from real commitments. After 
\begin_inset Formula $n|E|$
\end_inset

 trials, failure has negligible probability. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Succinctness and Zero Knowledge by Composition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(P,V)$
\end_inset

 be a public-coin interactive proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead of sending plaintext prover messages 
\begin_inset Formula $a_i$
\end_inset

, send commitments 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
c_i=\operatorname{Com}(k_i,a_i).
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

The commitments cannot simply be opened, since this would reveal the transcript. Instead, prove in zero knowledge that valid accepting openings exist.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Define the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{NP}$
\end_inset

 language 
\begin_inset Formula $L_V$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{multline*}
(r_1,\ldots,r_n,c_1,\ldots,c_n)\in L_V\\
\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists k_1,\ldots,k_n,a_1,\ldots,a_n
\end{multline*}
\end_inset

such that, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
c_i=\operatorname{Com}(k_i,a_i),
\end{equation*}
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation*}
V(r_1,\ldots,r_n,a_1,\ldots,a_n)=1.
\end{equation*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The prover and verifier then run a zero-knowledge proof that the public random coins and commitments belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $L_V$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Soundness and Proofs of Knowledge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To prove soundness, one would like to transform a cheating compiled prover 
\begin_inset Formula $P^*_{\mathrm{ZK}}$
\end_inset

 into a cheating prover for the original argument. The ordinary zero-knowledge proof guarantees only that accepting openings exist, not that they can be efficiently found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The required stronger property is a proof of knowledge. In the graph 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-coloring protocol, if 
\begin_inset Formula $P^*$
\end_inset

 convinces the verifier with probability greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $1-|E|^{-1}$
\end_inset

, then rewinding the prover and asking all possible edge challenges extracts valid openings for an entire coloring.
\end_layout

